<header><p class="st-kicker">county · France and the Low Countries</p><h1 class="st-title">County of Valentinois</h1><p class="st-meta">1100 CE – 1419 CE (approx.) · capital Valence</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Rhône valley county around Valence and Die held by the Poitiers family, later turned into a duchy for Cesare Borgia and Diane de Poitiers.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In the early 12th century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poitiers">Poitiers</a> family, of uncertain origin, established itself as counts in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valentinois">Valentinois</a>, the lands around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valence">Valence</a> on the left bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhone">Rhône</a>. By the 13th century they had also acquired the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diois">Diois</a> to the east, forming the united county of Valentinois and Diois.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The counts ruled a land of river trade, vineyards and Alpine valleys, holding their lands within the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-arles">Kingdom of Arles</a>. Much of their energy went into long struggles with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valence">Valence</a> and Die, who claimed lordship over the cities, and with the neighbouring dauphins of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/dauphine-of-viennois">Viennois</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The last count, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-ii-of-poitiers">Louis II of Poitiers</a>, bequeathed the county to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dauphin-of-france">Dauphin of France</a>, and it passed to the crown in 1419 despite claims by <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-savoy">Savoy</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xii-of-france">Louis XII</a> later made it a duchy for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cesare-borgia">Cesare Borgia</a> in 1498, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-ii-of-france">Henry II</a> gave it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diane-de-poitiers">Diane de Poitiers</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/county-of-valentinois.jpg" alt="County of Valentinois" width="320" height="352"><figcaption>Image: Wikimedia Commons · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Blason_maison_fr_de_Poitiers-Valentinois.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1100 CE – 1419 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>county</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Valence</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Aymar IV of Poitiers, Louis II of Poitiers</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Occitan</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/france">France</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Valentinois and Diois</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1260&amp;focus=county-of-valentinois" aria-label="County of Valentinois on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4796 591 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where County of Valentinois lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5116 847L5229 694M5116 847L5054 728"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5229 694h0M5054 728h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5116 847h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5116 847h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Valence. <a href="/atlas?year=1260&amp;focus=county-of-valentinois">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
